  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1146 PM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nToday through Thursday night\n\nAn anomalous 500 mb ridge will remain in control of the area's\nweather through Thursday night. This high will provide dry\nweather and unseasonably warm temperatures. High temperatures \nwill warm from the mid- and upper 60s today and be in the lower \nand mid-70s on Thursday. Low temperatures will be in the 30s and\n40s tonight and Wednesday night and in the 40s and 50s on \nThursday night.\n\nFriday and Friday night\n\nA negatively tilted trough will move through Minnesota and\nwestern and northern Wisconsin. There is still plenty of\nuncertainty on whether there will be enough low-level moisture\nfor any rain to move into our area.\n\nWith some more cover, high temperatures will be a bit cooler \n(mainly in the 60s). Low temperatures will be a bit warmer (upper\n40s to mid-50s).\n\nWeekend\n\nAnother negatively tilted trough will move through the region.\nThis one will be further southeast and it will drag a cold front\nsoutheast through the area. This will provide a slight chance\n(up to 24 percent) of rain.\n\nHigh temperatures will be mainly in the 60s. Low temperatures\nwill range from the upper 40s to mid-50s.\n\nEarly Next Week\n\nThere is plenty of spread in the models early next week. Some \nshow that the upper level ridge will build back north across the\narea. Other models show that the ridge will either flatten or \nretrograde west as a broad upper level trough moves through the \nregion. Due to this, there is much uncertainty on temperatures \nand rain chances.",
